                <pb facs="0245" n="238" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg238"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/8800: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">at
                        Nanking</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-21T18:00:00-04:00">July 21, 1937—6 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>111. Your telegram No. 287, July 16, 3 p.m., paragraph 3.</p>
                <p>The Department feels that your reply probably served a useful diplomatic purpose.
                    However, for your own orientation the Department points out that according to
                    the Neutrality Act that Act becomes applicable “whenever the President shall
                    find that there exists a state of war between, or among, two or more foreign
                    states.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
